WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.260
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.319
A problem solving website that teaches you
to think like an engineer.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:12.040
Tesla has grown rapidly over the past decade,
when it became the first American automotive

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.860
company to go public since Ford in 1956.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.880
The attraction towards Tesla is undeniable.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.670
Their cars are slick, their acceleration is
insane and perhaps most importantly, their

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.580
brand represents a movement towards renewable
energy.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.080
Tesla has attracted thousands of well intentioned
people who want to play their part in saving

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:38.079
the world, but there have been a niggling
questions on the minds of many EV owners and

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.870
EV naysayers.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:45.000
When is that expensive battery going to need
to be replaced, and at what cost.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.510
As existing Teslas begin to age, and more
exotic and demanding models of Teslas come

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.010
to the fore, like the Tesla Truck and the
Roadster 2.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.320
These issues are going to become more prominent,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.170
These batteries do NOT come cheap, but they
are getting cheaper.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:05.820
This chart shows the cost per kilowatt hour
for Tesla powerpacks, and the market average.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.900
Both dropping dramatically as technology advanced,
and manufacturing volumes increased.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.959
But that storage capacity slowly creeps away
as the battery is used, slowly degrading the

00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:18.350
range of your electric vehicle.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.759
Tesla currently offers a warranty to all Model
3 owners that cover it below 8 years or 160,000

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:27.280
kilometres, whichever comes first.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.579
Guaranteeing a retention of capacity of at
least 70% when used under normal use.

00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:36.090
If it falls below that, they will replace
your battery for free.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.789
Finding out what is considered normal use
is pretty difficult, but they seem to be reasonable

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:43.970
with it going by customer satisfaction reports.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:49.770
From our graph earlier, it’s estimated that
Tesla is achieving a cost of 150$ per kwH

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:56.020
of battery packs, so the 50 kWh battery pack
of the base model would cost around 7,500

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.659
dollars to replace, so they must be pretty
confident on those numbers.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:05.759
As a massive recall of the approximately 193
thousand Model 3s currently shipped would

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:06.950
ruin Tesla.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.380
[3] Ultimately these batteries are unlikely
to drop below the warranties guarantee in

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:17.709
those 160,000 kilometres, but even so improving
batteries is obviously just a wise business

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:20.769
decision to retain those customers in future.

00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:25.989
This is just one of a myriad of factors that
influenced Tesla’s recent landmark acquisition

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:30.520
of Maxwell Technologies for $218 million dollars.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.730
A rare Tesla acquisition that sets Tesla up
for not just cheaper batteries, but better

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.730
batteries.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.900
That will be lighter, have greater range,
and live a longer life.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.110
It wouldn’t be the first time an automotive
company underestimated their battery degradation.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:49.430
When the Nissan Leaf debuted in 2010, the
battery production they needed simply did

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:54.709
not exist, and neither did the technical expertise
required to design battery packs.

00:02:54.709 --> 00:03:00.650
In those days lithium ion batteries cost about
400 dollars per kWh for laptop grade batteries,

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:06.560
and up to 1000 dollars per kWh for ones with
the longevity needed for an electric vehicle.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.069
To minimise costs Nissan decided to start
production of their own batteries, and opted

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:17.340
for a small 24 kWh battery, giving it a range
of just over 100 kilometres.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.120
Suitable for city driving, and that’s about
it.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.190
But customers soon realised that this paltry
range was dwindling quickly.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:30.890
Within just 1-2 years of driving, the Leafs
battery capacity was dropping up to 27.5 percent

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.090
under normal use.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.560
[4] Despite careful in-house testing Nissan
overlooked some crucial test conditions when

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.739
developing their battery, and because of this
they made some crucial design errors.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:47.769
To learn why this degradation happens, we
first need to understand how lithium ion batteries

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:48.769
work.

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:53.129
A lithium ion battery, like all batteries,
contains a positive electrode, the anode,

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:57.390
and a negative electrode, the cathode, separated
by an electrolyte.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:03.010
Batteries power devices by transporting positively
charged ions between the anode and cathode,

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:07.120
creating an electric potential between the
two sides of the battery and forcing electrons

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.389
to travel through the device it is powering
to equalise the electric potential.

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:16.430
Critically, this process is reversible for
lithium ion batteries, as the lithium ions

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:21.500
are held loosely, sitting into spaces in the
anode and cathodes crystal structure.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:22.980
This is called intercalation.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:28.650
So, when the opposite electric potential is
applied to the battery it will force the lithium

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.570
ions to transport back across the electrolyte
bridge and lodge themselves in the anode once

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.570
again.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:39.790
This process determines a huge amount of the
energy storage capabilities of the battery.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:44.890
Lithium is a fantastic material for batteries,
with an atomic number of 3, it is the 3rd

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.750
lightest element and the lightest of the metals.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:52.370
Allowing it’s ions to provide fantastic
energy to weight characteristics for any battery.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:58.160
But, the energy capacity of the battery is
not determined by this, it is determined by

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.190
how many lithium ions can fit into these spaces
in the anode and cathode.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:08.850
For example, the graphite anode requires 6
carbon atoms to store a single lithium ion,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.720
to form this molecule (LiC6).

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:17.320
This gives a theoretical maximum battery capacity
of 372 mAh per gram.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.780
Silicon however can do better.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.730
A single silicon atom can bind 4.4 lithium
ions, giving it a theoretical maximum battery

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:30.380
capacity 4200mAh per gram.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:35.630
This seems great, and can provide increases
in battery capacity, but it also comes with

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:36.630
drawbacks.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.490
As those 4.4 lithium ions lodging themselves
into the silicon crystal lattice causes a

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.080
volume expansion of 400% when charging from
empty to full.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.650
This expansion creates stress within the battery
that damages the anode material, that will

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.900
eventually destroy it’s battery capacity
over repeated cycles.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:59.660
Battery designers are constantly looking for
ways to maximise this energy density of their

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.370
batteries while not sacrificing longevity
of the battery.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:09.430
So what exactly is being damaged in the batteries
that causes them to slowly wither away?

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:14.750
When researchers began investigating what
caused the Nissan Leaf’s rapid battery degradation,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.270
they began by opening the battery and unrolling
the batteries contents.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:24.080
They found that the electrode coatings had
become coarse over their life, clearly a non-reversible

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.360
reaction was occurring within the cell, the
change was expected.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.610
In fact the chemical process that caused it
is vital to the operation of the battery.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:38.550
When a battery is charged for the very first
time a chemical reaction occurs at the electrolyte

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:42.240
electrode interface, where electrons and ions
combine.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.020
This causes the formation of a new layer between
the electrode and electrolyte called the solid

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.820
electrolyte interphase.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:54.550
The name is exactly what it suggests, it’s
a layer formed by the liquid electrolyte reacting

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.280
with electrons to form a solid layer.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.520
Thankfully, this layer is permeable to ions,
but not electrons.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.370
So it initially forms a protective layer over
the electrode that allows ions to enter and

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:11.460
insert themselves via intercalation, but it
is impermeable to electrons.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.030
[10] Preventing further reaction with the
electrolyte.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.250
At least that’s the idea under normal conditions.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.250
[11]

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:22.640
The problem is, under certain conditions this
layer can grow beyond just a thin layer of

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.300
protective coating, and result in the permanent
lodgement of the lithium that provides the

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.450
battery with its energy storage.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:34.460
This process is not entirely well understood
and is outside the scope of this video, but

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:39.210
we can identify some factors that increase
the rate of this formation.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:44.080
The expansion of the silicon electrode battery
we mentioned earlier causes the fracture of

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:50.110
the SEI layer, exposing fresh layers of electrode
to react with the electrolyte.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:54.200
Charging rate and temperature can also accelerate
the thickening of this layer.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:00.190
NASA performed their own in depth study of
this effect, and released a report in 2008

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:05.510
titled “Guidelines on Lithium-ion Battery
Use in Space Applications” sharing their

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:06.510
findings.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:07.510
[12]

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:11.260
The temperature that the battery is charged
and discharged at plays a massive role in

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.270
the batteries performance.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:18.160
Lowering the temperature lowers chemical activity,
but this is a double edged sword.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.790
Lowering the chemical activity negatively
affects the batteries ability to store energy.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:26.850
Which is why batteries have lower ranges in
cold countries, but lowering the chemical

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:31.460
activity also decreases the formation rate
of that SEI layer.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:36.430
This is on of reason the Nissan Leaf’s battery
lost a huge amount of capacity over just 2

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.330
years in many countries.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:43.750
Nissan performed most of its testing in stable
laboratory conditions, not over a range of

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.560
possible temperatures.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.720
Because of this they failed to realise the
disastrous effect temperature would have on

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:54.220
the life of the battery, and failed to include
a thermal management system, which is common

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.190
place in any Tesla.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.730
This of course reduces the energy density
of the battery.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:04.200
Adding tubing, the glycol needed to exchange
heat, along with the heat pumps and valves

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:09.050
needed to make a thermal management system,
not only adds weight, but it draws energy

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.890
away from the battery to operate.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:15.230
But it plays a vital part in maintaining the
performance of the battery.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:20.540
Nissan’s choice to not include a thermal
management system, even in the 2019 version,

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:25.430
makes it a poor choice for anyone living in
anything but a temperate climate.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:30.080
Ofcourse, just cycling the battery though
it’s charged and discharged states is one

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.890
of the biggest factor in degrading the battery.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:38.130
Every time you cycle the battery you are giving
the SEI layer opportunities to grow.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.510
Minimising the number of times a cell is cycled
will increase it’s life, and maintaining

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:47.940
an ideal charge and discharge voltage of about
4 volts minimises any resistive heating that

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.330
may cause an increase in chemical activity.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.770
This is where Maxwell technologies comes into
play.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:59.080
Maxwell has two primary technologies that
Tesla will be taking advantage of.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:03.810
The first is what Maxwell are known for, their
ultracapacitors.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.181
Ultracapacitors serve the save fundamental
job as batteries, to store energy, but they

00:10:08.181 --> 00:10:13.540
function in an entirely different way and
are used for entirely different purposes.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:18.120
The fundamental difference between a capacitor
and a battery is that a battery stores energy

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.280
through chemical reactions, as we saw for
lithium ion batteries earlier this is done

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.560
through insertion into the crystal lattice.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.610
Capacitors instead store their energy by ions
clinging onto the surface of the electrode.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:34.850
This is a standard ultracapacitor schematic.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:39.490
On each side we have an aluminium current
collector with thin graphite electrodes on

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:45.440
each, separated by an electrolyte and an insulating
separator to prevent the passage of electrons.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.940
In an uncharged state ions float in the electrolyte.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:53.790
When a voltage is applied during charging,
ions drift towards their opposite charge and

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.320
cling to the surface, holding the charge in
place.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:01.779
When a device is then connected to the capacitor
this charge can quickly leave while the ions

00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:04.120
drift back into the electrolyte.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.970
The key limiting factor for ultracapacitors
is the surface area available for this to

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.920
happen, and nanotechnology has allowed for
amazing advances in the field.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.470
This is what the inside of a ultracapacitor
looks like, it contains hundreds of layers

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.050
of these electrode pairs.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.860
But even with this enormous surface area,
ultracapacitors simply cannot compete with

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:26.770
batteries when it comes to energy density.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:33.140
Even Maxwell’s best ultracapacitors have
an energy density of just 7.4 Wh/kg [13] while

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:37.810
the best guess for Tesla’s current energy
density is about 250 Wh/kg.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:43.400
Counter to what corporate owned tech channels
may tell you, ultracapacitors are not intended

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.350
to be a replacement for batteries.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:49.700
They are intended to work in conjunction with
batteries.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:53.310
Ultracapacitors strength is their ability
to quickly charge and discharge without being

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:54.460
worn down.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:58.680
This makes them a great buffer to place between
the motors and the battery.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.930
Their high discharge rate will allow them
to give surges of electricity to the motors

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:08.110
when rapid acceleration is needed, and allow
them to charge quickly when breaking.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:12.400
Saving the battery from unnecessary cycles
and boosting its ability to quickly provide

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:14.980
current when needed for acceleration.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:19.810
This is going to be a massively important
technology for two upcoming Tesla vehicles.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:26.870
The Tesla Roadster, which will boast an acceleration
of 0-60 in just 1.9 seconds, which a normal

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:31.260
battery would struggle to achieve the discharge
rate needed without damaging itself.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.700
The second vehicle is the Tesla Truck.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:38.200
I have made a video in the past noting that
the Tesla Truck is going to be limited in

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:43.650
its range and cargo hauling ability as a result
of the heavy batteries it will need, as trucks

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:47.950
are limited in weight to about 40 metric tonnes
in most countries.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:53.020
This ultracapacitor technology will boost
its ability to regain energy from breaking

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:58.060
significantly, and thus allow its battery
capacity to decrease, in turn allowing the

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.110
truck to swap batteries for cargo.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:05.970
The second technology Maxwell has been toting
as their next big breakthrough is dry coated

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:06.970
batteries.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:11.710
[9] This is a manufacturing advancement that
Maxwell claims will reduce the cost of manufacturing.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:17.260
A factor Tesla has been working fervently
to minimize with the growth of the gigafactory.

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:20.089
So what are dry coated batteries.

00:13:20.089 --> 00:13:25.070
Currently in order to coat their current collectors
with the electrode material Tesla, in partnership

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:29.510
with Panasonic’s patented technology, must
use first dissolve the electrode material

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:34.390
in a solvent which is then spread over current
collector, both are then passed through an

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:39.800
oven for drying, where the solvent evaporates
leaving just the electrode material behind.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:44.740
This adds cost of the manufacturing procedure
as the solvent is lost in the process, and

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:46.850
the baking process takes energy.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:52.830
On top of this the solvent is toxic, so removing
it from the process would benefit the environment.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:58.500
Maxwell instead uses a binding agent and conductive
agent, which I assume will work similarly

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.260
to electrostatic painting.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:04.029
Where a metal being painted will be given
a negative charge, while the paint will be

00:14:04.029 --> 00:14:09.860
given a positive charge as it is sprayed attracting
it to the metal where it will cling to it.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:13.470
This painting process also eliminates the
solvents needed in paint.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:18.910
In this paper, published by Maxwell technologies,
they detail how their dry coating manufacturing

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:24.480
techniques could result in a high energy storage
capacity of the electrodes, due to a denser

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.170
and thicker coating.

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:31.800
Resulting a potential increase in battery
capacity to 300 Watt hours per kilogram, 20%

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:35.410
up from our best estimates of Tesla’s current
specs.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:39.860
Only time will tell if this claim can be realised
at an industrial scale.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:44.839
Perhaps, more importantly to Tesla, they now
own this manufacturing technique.

00:14:44.839 --> 00:14:48.800
Currently Panasonic owns the manufacturing
process for Tesla, there is a literally a

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:54.330
line of demarcation in the gigafactory separating
Panasonic and Tesla, denoting the point at

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.550
which the ownership of batteries transfers
hands.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:02.200
Having to buy their batteries from Panasonic
adds cost, that Tesla will want to avoid in

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:07.779
future and this step could allow for full
vertical integration of their battery manufacturing.

00:15:07.779 --> 00:15:12.839
Thereby making electronic vehicles more affordable
to the everyday consumer.

00:15:12.839 --> 00:15:18.380
All of this technology is powered by incredibly
smart engineers working to solve really interesting

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:23.100
problems, and with so much focus on battery
technology across the entire tech industry

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.450
there’s a high demand for qualified engineers.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:31.070
For anyone looking to build or advance their
engineering career I’d highly recommend

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.070
Brilliant.

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:36.080
Brilliant recently introduced a new feature,
called “Daily Problems”, which will present

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:40.420
with you with interesting scientific and mathematical
problems to test your brain.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.310
Like this one, that teaches you about rolling
resistance.

00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:45.510
One of the ways vehicles lose energy.

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:50.100
It takes you through a short explanation of
rolling resistance, giving you the framework

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:53.100
you need to rationalise a question they pose
to you.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.089
Here the answer is pretty simple.

00:15:55.089 --> 00:16:00.100
Rolling resistance occurs from the loss of
energy to the ground and wheels, so by driving

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:03.870
from gravel to concrete we lose less energy
to the ground.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:08.110
If you answer a question wrong though, you
